building yupiter
=================

Requirements
------------

  * GNU Make 3.82 or newer.  The Makefile uses simply-expanded
    variables, pattern rules and "-include"; a POSIX make is not
    enough.
  * A POSIX shell, sed, awk, install, nm and readelf.  They are used by
    ./configure and by the test scripts; no CMake, Python or generator
    is needed at any point.
  * GNU or Clang C and C++ compilers (the flags are GNU-style and are
    only used after the compiler has been identified).
  * A C++23 compiler whose standard library supports freestanding
    mode: the implementation includes "<climits>", "<cstdarg>",
    "<cstddef>" and "<cstdint>" with "-ffreestanding -fno-exceptions
    -fno-rtti".  libstdc++ provides that from GCC 13 on; libc++ and the
    MSVC standard library do not implement freestanding mode, and
    ./configure fails with a message that names what is missing
    instead of quietly building a hosted program.
  * Linux on aarch64 or on x86_64.  Any other operating system or
    architecture is rejected at configure time rather than silently
    mapped onto an implementation that does not match it.

No network access, no package manager and no third-party dependency
is needed at any point.


Quick start
-----------

    ./configure
    make
    make check
    make install

"make" builds both libyupiter.a and libyupiter.so by default.  The
build tree is always "build" in the source directory: it is removed by
"make clean" and ignored by Git.  ./configure detects the target and
writes config.mk, which every later make invocation includes; running
./configure again is the only way to change an option.

    make static       libyupiter.a only
    make shared       libyupiter.so only
    make check        build and run the test suite
    make install      install headers, libraries and package metadata
    make uninstall    remove the installed files
    make distclean    remove the build tree and config.mk


Configure options
-----------------

./configure accepts the options below; ./configure --help prints the
same list.

--prefix=PATH
    Installation prefix, /usr/local by default.  It must be absolute.

--bindir=DIR, --libdir=DIR, --includedir=DIR
    Installation directories relative to the prefix: bin, lib and
    include by default.  They must be relative, because the installed
    CMake package derives the prefix from its own location.

--arch=ARCH
    Target architecture: aarch64 or x86_64.  It is detected from
    uname when the option is not given.

--cc=CC, --cxx=CXX
    C and C++ compilers, "cc" and "c++" by default.  The CXX
    environment variable is honoured when the option is not given, as
    are CC, AR, RANLIB, NM, READELF and INSTALL.

--build-type=TYPE
    debug (-O0 -g3), release (-O2 -DNDEBUG), relwithdebinfo (-O2 -g
    -DNDEBUG) or minsize (-Os -DNDEBUG).  RelWithDebInfo when the
    option is not given, so a default build is optimised and still
    carries debug information.

--enable-static=yes|no
    Build libyupiter.a.  Yes by default.

--enable-shared=yes|no
    Build libyupiter.so.  Yes by default.  At least one of the two
    libraries must be enabled.

--enable-tests=yes|no
    Build the test programs.  Yes by default.  The tests link the
    static archive, so they require --enable-static=yes.

--heap-size=BYTES
    Size in bytes of the dynamic memory arena (4096 by default).  It
    reaches the allocator as a compile definition, so the configured
    value is the only one the library is built with; it reaches the
    test programs the same way, so none of them is built against a
    different value from the library it links.

    The smallest arena that can work is 64 bytes: every block carries a
    header of its own, and an arena that cannot hold one block and the
    payload of another can serve no program at all.  The configure
    guard rejects anything below 64, and anything that is not a plain
    decimal number, when the project is configured rather than when it
    runs.

    The tests do not depend on the value: they ask the allocator what
    it has and shape each scenario to fit, so the same sources pass from
    64 bytes upwards, and a smaller heap only makes a leak show up
    sooner.

For example:

    ./configure --build-type=debug --heap-size=1024
    ./configure --prefix="$HOME/.local"
    ./configure --enable-shared=no
    ./configure --arch=aarch64 --cc=aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc \
                --cxx=aarch64-linux-gnu-g++


How the flags are chosen
------------------------

The library is compiled as freestanding C++23 and the tests are not,
for reasons that matter:

    library    -std=c++23 <C++ warning set> -ffreestanding -fno-builtin
               -fno-stack-protector -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti
               -fno-threadsafe-statics -fPIC -fvisibility=hidden

    C tests    -std=c17 <C warning set> -fno-builtin -fno-stack-protector

    C++ test   -std=c++17 <C++ warning set>

    link       -nostdlib -static

  * "-ffreestanding" is deliberately not used for the test programs:
    it makes clang treat main() as an ordinary function, mangles its
    name and removes the implicit "return 0", so the program would
    exit with whatever the last call left in the return register.
  * "-fno-builtin" stops a call from being rewritten into a compiler
    builtin that this library does not provide.
  * "-fno-stack-protector" removes the need for a failure handler
    symbol that does not exist here.
  * "-fno-exceptions", "-fno-rtti" and "-fno-threadsafe-statics" keep
    the C++ runtime out of the library: no unwinder, no personality
    routine, no type information and no guard variables for
    function-local statics.  An object that would need dynamic
    initialization is rejected while the library is compiled - the
    stream objects and the heap arena are "constinit", which is
    exactly the promise the freestanding entry point needs, since it
    runs no constructors.
  * "-nostdlib -static" makes the library's own _start, its printf and
    its string helpers the only ones available at link time, which is
    the point of the whole exercise.  "-static" is also what keeps the
    result a plain non-PIE executable: both drivers already imply
    "-no-pie" for a static link, and passing it as well only makes
    Clang report it as an unused argument on every link.
  * "-fPIC" is used for the objects of both libraries, so a static
    archive can also be linked into a position-independent executable,
    which matters on aarch64.
  * "-fvisibility=hidden" and YUPITER_PUBLIC make the shared object
    export the documented C interface and nothing else.  The public
    header marks every exported declaration; internal helpers stay
    hidden.
  * The default RelWithDebInfo build type contributes "-O2 -g
    -DNDEBUG".  The library itself has no assert calls, so -DNDEBUG
    changes nothing in the archive; -O2 is what lets the block copies
    in io/string.cxx become wide loads and stores, and -g keeps a
    failure readable in a debugger.

The same warning set is used for the library and for the C tests, so a
test can never be sloppier than the code it exercises:

    -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror
    -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow
    -Wstrict-prototypes -Wmissing-prototypes -Wcast-qual
    -Wundef -Wswitch-default -Wdouble-promotion -Wvla

The C++ sources use the same set without the two prototype options,
which only exist for C, and with four additions that keep a C++
implementation honest:

    -Wold-style-cast -Wnon-virtual-dtor -Woverloaded-virtual
    -Wzero-as-null-pointer-constant

Every one of them is understood by both GNU C++ and Clang, so no check
is made before they are passed.  The format check stays enabled for
every test program; the few cases that pin down behaviour the standard
leaves undefined, or that the check rejects, are called through a
function pointer in the test source, for a reason that is written down
where it applies.  See docs/testing.

The public header and the C test programs are built as C17 with
extensions off, and the implementation is built as C++23, also with
extensions off.  The C++ compatibility test is built as C++17 on
purpose: the archive is meant to be consumable from an older C++ as
well, and the test proves it.

A static library is never linked on its own, so the properties above
are checked on the archive itself: the archive_is_freestanding test
inspects every member for references to a C library or a C++ runtime,
for exported mangled names and for sections that run code before the
program.  The shared object is inspected the same way, and it must
also have no undefined dynamic symbols and no NEEDED entry.  See
docs/testing.


Static and shared libraries
---------------------------

Both libraries are built from the same object set, so their code is
identical; only the link step differs.

libyupiter.a is the freestanding archive.  It contains the startup file
of the target, so a program linked with "-nostdlib" gets its entry
point from it.  A hosted program that links the archive does not pull
that member in, because nothing refers to "_start" in such a program,
and its own C runtime keeps providing the entry point.

libyupiter.so.1.0.1 is the hosted shared object.  It excludes the
startup file, carries SONAME libyupiter.so.1 and the usual two links
(name and soname), and names no shared library it needs: no C library
and no C++ runtime.  It is loaded by a program that already has an
entry point.


Installation
------------

The default prefix is /usr/local, which normally needs the privileges
of the root user:

    ./configure
    make
    sudo make install

A per-user installation needs no privileges and puts everything below
the home directory:

    ./configure --prefix="$HOME/.local"
    make
    make install

The prefix belongs on the configure line, not on the install line: the
paths written into yupiter.pc are fixed when the project is
configured.

"make install" honours DESTDIR for a package builder:

    make install DESTDIR=/tmp/stage

Installed are the two headers (yupiter.h and yupiter.hpp), the static
archive, the shared object with its two links, the pkg-config file and
the CMake package configuration.


Consuming the library
---------------------

With pkg-config:

    cc my_program.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs yupiter)

A prefix outside the search path of pkg-config, such as the home
directory, is not looked in by default:

    PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$HOME/.local/lib/pkgconfig" \
        cc my_program.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs yupiter)

With CMake (the package configuration is installed whether or not
CMake was used to build the library):

    find_package(yupiter REQUIRED)
    target_link_libraries(my_program PRIVATE yupiter::yupiter)

pointing CMake at the prefix with -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=<prefix>.
yupiter::yupiter is the shared library when the package provides one
and the static library otherwise; yupiter::yupiter_shared and
yupiter::yupiter_static name a particular kind.

The archive is linked the same way from C and from C++: it exports the
C interface, refers to nothing outside itself except the program's
main, and needs no C++ runtime, so a C program can link it with a C
driver and a C++ program needs no C++23 support of its own - the C++
compatibility test is built as C++17 to prove that.

Note that the library is freestanding by design: a program linked
without a C runtime needs the entry point that yupiter provides, and
one linked with a normal C runtime gets its entry point from there
instead.

One detail of linking without a C runtime is worth knowing before it is
met the hard way, because the symptom - the program dies with SIGBUS
before main() has done anything - says nothing about the cause.  GNU ld
places the read-write segment at a file offset congruent to its virtual
address modulo the maximum page size of the target (64 KiB on aarch64,
4 KiB on x86_64), and it pads the file out to that offset only when it
has something to write there.  A static
executable with nothing file-backed in its read-write segment - no
global object with an initializer, nothing the linker has to write into
the image - therefore has that segment placed past the end of the file,
the kernel maps the page from there, and the first access to the .bss
that follows it is reported as SIGBUS.  One initialized object anywhere
in the program is enough to prevent it, and a program that uses
yupiter_stdout or yupiter_stderr already has several.  Linking with
"-z max-page-size=4096" avoids it as well, at the cost of executables
that a kernel with 64 KiB pages may refuse to load; the test suite
prefers the initialized object.  test/largewrite.c records the same
thing where it applies.


Continuous integration
----------------------

The workflows under .github/workflows/ build and test the library on
native runners, so nothing there is cross-compiled: the matrix has rows
on x86_64 runners and rows on aarch64 runners.  On every push and pull
request it checks:

  * the build and the test suite under GNU C and under Clang, with
    RelWithDebInfo for the ordinary rows, Debug for the smallest-arena
    row and Release for the static-only row, at the default arena size,
    at the smallest one the configure script accepts and at an
    intermediate size, so the boundary the allocator documents is
    exercised as well as the ordinary case;
  * the static library, the shared library and both, so a configuration
    that disables one of them is still built and inspected; the
    shared-only rows verify the object, its SONAME and its exports
    because the test programs link the static archive;
  * test/expected-format.txt regenerated from the host C library on
    both architectures, which fails if the golden file in the
    repository no longer matches what a reference library prints, so it
    cannot drift into being a snapshot of yupiter;
  * the configure guard, which has to reject the arena sizes, the build
    types, the library combinations and the architectures it documents
    as impossible, and accept the alias spellings of the implemented
    architecture.

Every step the workflow runs can be run by hand with the commands in
this document and in docs/testing.


Porting
-------

The platform-specific pieces are chosen in exactly two places, and a
new operating system or architecture has to satisfy both:

  1. ./configure decides what is supported.  It accepts Linux on
     aarch64 (aarch64, arm64, ARM64) and Linux on x86_64 (x86_64,
     amd64, AMD64) and fails with a clear message for anything else,
     so an unimplemented target can never silently pick an incompatible
     implementation.  The processor it selects also names the assembly
     files of the target.

  2. The implementation itself:
       assembly/yupiter_start_<processor>.S    program entry point,
       assembly/yupiter_syscall_<processor>.S  six-argument system-call
                                               trampoline,
       assembly/yupiter_syscall3_<processor>.S three-argument
                                               system-call trampoline,
       io/syscall_linux.cxx                    system-call numbers, one
                                               block per architecture.

A port adds the files for its target, teaches the configure script about
them and adds the architecture's system-call numbers to
io/syscall_linux.cxx, keeping every operating-system-specific and
architecture-specific value out of the portable code in io/ and mem/.
The system-call layer is the only place where numbers and register
conventions appear.

Cross-compilation is possible by naming the target and the cross
compiler together, for example:

    ./configure --arch=aarch64 \
        --cc=aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc --cxx=aarch64-linux-gnu-g++

It is not claimed to work until a toolchain for the target has built
the library and its tests successfully.
